Kagu's Cooking Tips for OS Chefs
=================================
1. Always initialize your variables before cooking
2. Never dereference a null pointer in the kitchen
3. Use mutexes when multiple chefs share the same pan
4. Free your memory after every meal service
5. Handle all error codes - unhandled exceptions spoil the broth
6. Keep your stack clean - overflow ruins the souffle
7. Cache your most popular recipes for faster service
8. Use pipes to pass ingredients between stations
9. Always close your file descriptors after reading recipes
10. A good chef handles SIGTERM gracefully
11. Never run the kitchen as root unless necessary
12. Log everything - debugging a cold kitchen is impossible
Kagu Restaurant Glossary
=========================
Fork    - When a dish splits into two identical copies
Pipe    - How ingredients flow between kitchen stations
Signal  - The bell the waiter rings when food is ready
Daemon  - The night shift cook who never goes home
Zombie  - A finished dish that nobody picked up
Orphan  - A dish whose chef has left the kitchen
Socket  - Where you plug in the electric stove
Thread  - A helper cook sharing the same kitchen
Mutex   - The lock on the shared refrigerator
Kernel  - The head chef managing everything
Shell   - The front desk taking customer orders
Swap    - When you run out of counter space and use the floor
Boot    - The morning routine of turning everything on


























































































































































































































































